<!--
    An ARM hard-float function returns a double in d0 and a float in s0
    (option armfloatreturn, default off).

    The default model lists separate 4-byte s-registers. Wide returns need
    both return-word recovery and calling-convention evidence. The checks
    require correct enabled output, not a particular broken disabled output.

    The raw image below has no ELF container, so it states no calling
    convention: the option leaves it alone, and only an explicit prototype
    brings the double back.

    armfloatreturn_armhf.o (clang -mfloat-abi=hard, source beside it) is a
    relocatable object whose .ARM.attributes says Tag_ABI_VFP_args=1.
    fmtlf_armhf is a linked PIE whose ELF header carries the hard-float flag.
    Pass 1 (option off) checks shared integer-result controls; pass 2 (on)
    returns the whole double, takes the d0 parameter, narrows a float converted from
    a double to s0, keeps an integer return in r0 while d0 is also written
    or still holds a callee's double, fills an unused d0 below a used d1 with
    one double, does not make two floats in s0/s1 one 8-byte parameter, and
    returns a double that one path takes from a call and another computes.
